w some basic  asset statistics about the environment  and in the bottom right you can view the  asset utilization as a percentage of the  total allocated capacity on the left  side toolbar you can view additional  details about the operating systems  storage memory server age and the  installed software within the  environment of course you won't be able  to view this information until the scan  is actually running to view the progress  of the scan access the assessments tab  at the top after you click on  assessments you'll be presented with  your different assessment groups in this  example we have a group for East Coast  West Coast primary data center and  anything that's not Windows or Linux  which must be a manual asset entry as  you can see we have one collector for  each data center location the general  recommendation is to have one per  location but keep in mind that the  customer can install more than one per  location if desired and any duplicate  assets that are scanned by both  collectors will be automatically removed  by the system note that for each  collector you can check the status of  the scan as well as the server count of  the expected values for the status it  will either say complete or installed if  it's been started or it will say not  started if it has not been installed and  connected to the web service  after the collectors are installed and  configured you'll be able to view the  inventory as it comes in to do so  access the inventory tab at the top now  that we have accessed this specific  server at the top you can see all of the  basic configuration info this will tell  you not only what is the server name and  IP but also whether it's a virtual  machine or bare metal  how many CPU cores are allocated how  much RAM is allocated what operating  system it's running on as well as the  processor of the host machine if you  scroll down you can also view graphs  that outline the performance data or the  utilization of each of those components  over time similar to the summary tab  more information can be accessed on the  left-hand toolbar for instance clicking  on the storage tab will allow you to  view details about the exact storage  partitions what's free what's not if you  click on network you can view the exact  network configuration as well as what  subnet that server is located in you can  also view running services on that  server  running processes installed software if  there are any as well as memory  utilization metrics and CPU utilization  metrics by process what I have just  shown you from the inventory tab  represents all of the configuration and  performance data that we have collected  from each server the last piece of  information that we have are the network  dependencies to view the network  dependencies click on the dependency tab  at the top the analyze dependency tab  allows you to view server to server  network dependencies by selecting an  individual server to select that server  you can do so by accessing your  collector groups and a specific  collector or you can just click on  details on the left-hand toolbar to view  all available servers once you've  selected the server that you want click  on the eyeglasses symbol to view it this  will now provide you with a graphical  view  of the network dependencies as well as a  more specific view in a table format  below as you can see we're collecting  the protocol source and target IP source  and target port as well as the installed  app on the source machine all of this  information comes from in that stack  command and is included as part of the  agentless scan via strata probe one  critical feature of this tab is its  ability to detect unscanned devices you  can see an example of this down here  where it says on scan device if I expand  that you can see the same net stat  details about that one scan device you  can also view that device in a graphical  format up here there is a nun scan  device button that will bundle any  unscanned devices if you double click  that and click this in the top right to  need in the view you can see that there  are actually two distinct IP addresses  now we have not scanned these devices  and so the only thing that we know about  them are their IPs but this is still  nonetheless useful for a scoping  exercise to ensure that all target  servers are being included in the  analysis the next tab we will look at is  the strata fit table the strata fit tab  is a machine level indicator of cloud  readiness it does not take into account  broader server-side application  structure however it does look at each  individual server and determine based on  that servers capacity and operating  system whether it is ready for a lift  and shift to the cloud as you can see  the strata fit is represented by three  different scores low medium and high  high are the easy movers these are the  servers that are already modernized and  ready for lift and shift lows are  anything that is incompatible for the  cloud entirely usually this is due to an  older operating system say an old CentOS  version like CentOS 4 or 5 or Windows  2000 / 2003 that are deemed incompatible  for the cloud the servers that are  flagged medium are somewhere in between  where they are technically  enough for the cloud however they tend  to have a higher number of network  dependencies with other servers this  means that they are most likely involved  in some type of broader more complicated  app structure that may make it more  difficult to move if you would like to  investigate why particular server  received the score that it did you can  do so on the left-hand toolbar under  details if you click on details it will  list out all of the scored servers from  low to medium to high if you want to  know why a server received a score other  than high simply click on the drop down  for that server and view those reasons  in sentence format note that the strata  fit score has 6 different parameters you  can customize how sensitive each of  these parameters are on the left hand  toolbar under actions customize  parameters if you click on customize  parameters you will see all 6 of them  listed out and you can adjust this  slider bar to change the sensitivity of  each to finish up the analyze module  let's take a look at the reports tab  within the reports tab it's important to  note that any of the information  contained in the analyze module can be  exported as a report at the top our  summary reports which tend to be either  slideshows or PDFs at the bottom are  detailed reports which are essentially  CSV dumps of the raw information to  generate an executive summary report  slideshow create a summary report first  click on the button for that then select  the groups whether collectors or bill  groups that you would like to include in  the slideshow  you can then select your baseline vendor  catalog which we will usually use the  private data center compare that to the  various Google catalogs name it and  click create report after your report is  finished building you can download it by  clicking this icon note that you can  download it as a Google slides as well  if you prefer now let's open this up so  we can take a look at what is produced  so for starters it will produce some  introductory slides like a table of  contents as well as a background on the  overall assessment process it will also  produce a slide outlining the different  collectors that were set up  then it's going to jump right into the  high-level findings so this is the  high-level configuration here followed  by some more advanced metrics like the  utilisation percentage in the bottom  right and then jumping into the cloud  readiness so this is the strata fit  score which is an infrastructure cloud  readiness score and we also have a pads  fit score so this is going to identify  any potential pass candidates after  those high-level findings will show that  financial comparison and this is what  that's going to look like after covering  the financial comparison there will be a  couple of slides talking about the high  level next steps and this is intended to  lead into the next conversation for an  advanced service thanks so much for that  demo Carl really impressive and with  that being said I just wanted to recall  for everyone who's watching that you can  also do exactly what Carl just did in a  full hands-on lab environment prior to  deploying strata zone within your  environment to do so and take the  introduction to Stratus own assessments  hands-on lab follow the first link to  register her access to Google Cloud  learnings platforms and then click on  the following link so that you can start  the lab  actually leverage strata zone within a  real-world hands-on environment prior to  actually deploying it in your enterprise  environment with that being said Carl  and I would both like to thank you for  your time we look forward to seeing you  on Google Cloud and we hope you really  enjoy the benefits that Stratus own will  bring to your business thank you thank  you
 

